{
  "file": "crates/habeas-sdk/tests/storage.rs",
  "item": "impl[StoredGraph/Contents]/fn[fingerprint]",
  "citations": [
    {
      "role": "cite",
      "name": "habeas:coverage",
      "commentary": "These checks load and store recorded dependencies without checking their\n freshness. Fingerprinting is outside the storage and output-decoding\n operations being tested, so this guard must remain unused.\n Other domain fixtures exercise tracked fingerprint observations."
    }
  ],
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The `habeas:coverage` citation on `crates/habeas-sdk/tests/storage.rs#impl[StoredGraph/Contents]/fn[fingerprint]` is a valid coverage-exception proposal.\n\n- **Form:** The citation is a fenced commentary block on an explicit function with a body. This is where the built-in allows a function-level exception.\n- **Substance:** `fingerprint` is a guard that panics. `Contents` requires the method, but the storage tests don't need it. The commentary says it \"must remain unused\" because freshness checking and fingerprinting are outside what these tests cover. That reason is substantive: running the guard would be a test failure.\n- **Agreement with the source:** The tests that use `StoredGraph` are `records_round_trip_and_malformed_or_incompatible_data_is_an_error`, `malformed_records_are_storage_errors_and_bad_outputs_are_output_errors` and the delegation test. They only store and load records, decode outputs, and call `verify`. `StoredReview::execute` ignores its context, so it records no dependencies that would need fingerprinting. Nothing in them needs a fingerprint, which matches the commentary.\n- **Claim about other fixtures:** Other fixtures do implement real fingerprints and exercise them: `Memory` and `Application` in `agent.rs`, `contracts.rs` and `validation.rs`. That supports the claim that other fixtures exercise tracked fingerprint observations.\n- **Redundancy:** The item has no `{impl}` or `{test}` reference to `habeas:coverage`, so the citation is not redundant.\n\nI found no gap.",
